Use all uploaded images to determine the product’s appearance and merge information across all references into one unified, consistent product. Do not copy camera angles, lighting, backgrounds, staging, or arrangements from the reference images. Use them only to establish the product’s identity, geometry, proportions, construction, materials, surface finishes, color palette, branding, and functional details.

Depict one exact product model, variant, and configuration throughout the entire sheet. Do not combine incompatible features from different versions or colorways. If references conflict, prioritize features consistently supported by multiple images. For unseen areas, use conservative structural continuity without inventing decorative elements, branding, or functionality.

LAYOUT:
Arrange the product reference sheet as a clean studio presentation with nine evenly spaced panels. All views must depict the exact same product. Use a neutral, seamless background with generous spacing.

Keep the complete-product views at a consistent scale. Center the product within each panel and leave enough margin to show its entire silhouette. Detail panels may use greater magnification.

Establish a fixed front, rear, left, right, top, and bottom orientation for the product and preserve these relationships across all panels. Use orthographic or near-orthographic projection for straight-on views, with minimal perspective distortion.

[VIEW 1 – COMPLETE PRODUCT FRONT]
Straight-on front view. Show the entire product, including all attached components. Clearly reveal its primary silhouette, proportions, front-facing surfaces, branding, controls, openings, and construction details.

[VIEW 2 – COMPLETE PRODUCT LEFT PROFILE]
Exact 90-degree left side view. Show the entire product. Accurately preserve its depth, side silhouette, thickness, contours, protrusions, seams, and component alignment.

[VIEW 3 – COMPLETE PRODUCT REAR]
Straight-on rear view. Show the entire product. Clearly reveal the back construction, rear surfaces, attachment points, seams, closures, connectors, and any reference-supported branding or markings.

[VIEW 4 – COMPLETE PRODUCT REAR THREE-QUARTER]
Rear three-quarter view showing the rear and right side. Show the entire product. Reveal how surfaces, materials, edges, and components wrap around and connect between the front, sides, and back. Maintain accurate volume and proportions.

[VIEW 5 – COMPLETE PRODUCT TOP]
Direct overhead view of the product’s top. Show its complete outline from this direction. Clearly reveal the footprint, upper surfaces, openings, handles, controls, seams, and arrangement of components where applicable.

[VIEW 6 – COMPLETE PRODUCT BOTTOM]
Direct underside view. Show the complete outline of the base. Accurately depict reference-supported feet, pads, tread, fasteners, seams, vents, ports, labels, and structural details where applicable. Do not invent hidden mechanisms or unsupported underside features.

[VIEW 7 – SIGNATURE FEATURE DETAIL]
Extreme close-up of the product’s most distinctive identifying feature, such as its logo, emblem, pattern, control interface, lens, closure, or defining design element.

Preserve its exact shape, placement, proportions, colors, engraving, embossing, printing, and surrounding material. Reproduce product text only when clearly legible in the references. Do not invent words, symbols, or specifications.

[VIEW 8 – CONSTRUCTION & FUNCTIONAL DETAIL]
High-detail close-up of an important connection or functional area, such as a hinge, clasp, zipper, handle attachment, button, connector, strap, stitched joint, or assembled edge.

Show how the visible parts fit together, including their thickness, spacing, alignment, attachment method, and material transitions. Keep the product in the same configuration as the complete-product views. Do not introduce exploded views, cutaways, detached parts, or unsupported internal components.

[VIEW 9 – MATERIAL & SURFACE DETAIL]
Macro close-up showing the product’s characteristic materials and finishes, preferably where two materials meet.

Accurately preserve fabric weave, leather grain, plastic texture, metal finish, wood grain, glass transparency, coatings, stitching, seams, edge finishing, and reference-supported wear where applicable. Match the actual scale of the texture. Do not add artificial distressing, scratches, or exaggerated surface detail.

PRODUCT CONSISTENCY REQUIREMENTS:
All panels must depict the exact same physical product.

Geometry, dimensions, proportions, curvature, thickness, component count, seam placement, construction, colors, materials, finishes, branding, patterns, fasteners, openings, controls, and accessories must remain perfectly consistent across every panel.

Maintain the correct handedness and orientation of all asymmetric features. Do not mirror logos, reverse component placement, change patterns, move seams, duplicate controls, or alter the product between views.

Keep flexible elements in one consistent neutral arrangement. Keep movable components in the same position and operating state throughout the sheet.

Show only accessories or attachments established as part of the selected product configuration. Do not add packaging, display stands, styling props, or unrelated objects.

Use only details applicable to the referenced product. Examples listed in the panel instructions are options, not a request to add those features.

LIGHTING:
Clean studio lighting. Soft key light from upper left, gentle fill light from the right. Even illumination across all panels. Neutral color balance and consistent exposure.

Use controlled reflections that reveal surface shape and finish without obscuring details. Preserve the true appearance of matte, glossy, metallic, translucent, and transparent materials. Avoid dramatic shadows, colored lighting, excessive glare, and stylized reflections.

PRESENTATION:
Highly detailed professional product reference sheet. Photorealistic materials. Sharp focus. Clean layout. Accurate geometry. Consistent scale and proportions. Faithful industrial design and construction.

Every complete-product view must show the entire product without cropping. Close-up panels may crop intentionally to reveal fine detail.

No added text, panel labels, captions, dimensions, measurement arrows, graphic overlays, watermarks, signatures, decorative elements, background scenery, people, hands, unrelated products, or props outside the product design.

Preserve original logos, labels, and markings that physically belong to the referenced product. Do not add new branding.
